ing shared record's `message` attribute — that's fine? Spec says cannot rewrite msg, args, exc_info, stack_info. `message` is derived; logging.Formatter.format also sets record.message = record.getMessage(). But if I set record.message to truncated, and format string uses %(message)s, then truncated text is used — but I must not mutate record for subsequent handlers... record.message is derived and other handlers' Formatter.format will recompute record.message = record.getMessage() (Python 3.8+ Formatter.format sets self.formatMessage → record.message = record.getMessage()). Actually logging.Formatter.format does: record.message = record.getMessage() then s = self.formatMessage(record). So each handler recomputes. Safe. But better not mutate: use a copy? formatMessage uses record. For safety, I can temporarily set record.message and restore in finally.\n\nSimplest: \n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        orig_message = getattr(record, \"message\", None)\n        try:\n            record.message = record.getMessage()\n            msg = self.formatMessage(record)   # applies fmt with args substituted\n            ...\n        finally:\n            restore\n```\nBut original code did `msg = super().format(record)` then used record.message for prefix. To limit code churn and keep exception handling: use super().format(record) after temporarily overriding record.message? super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() itself, overwriting. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: call super().format(record) first (gives full msg with exc_info appended). Truncating full output would include prefix and exception, not allowed.\n\nBetter approach: compute getMessage, truncate, then temporarily replace record.msg and args: set record.msg = truncated, record.args = () then call super().format? That mutates msg (forbidden but restored after). Risky given \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args...\" — restoring makes it not observable, but safest: use a shallow copy of the record.\n\nUse `copy.co